# emacs: -*- mode: python; py-indent-offset: 4; indent-tabs-mode: nil -*-
# vi: set ft=python sts=4 ts=4 sw=4 et:
"""Convert a PowerPoint slide into a layered SVG with links, plus svgtune instructions.

Each shape of the slide becomes a group in the SVG, labelled with the shape's
name (``inkscape:label``, as in PowerPoint's Selection Pane), with its text
kept as <text> (editable, e.g. in Inkscape) and its links as <a>.  Shapes are
grouped into Inkscape layers, one per build step, and a ``<output>.svgtune``
is written which shows the steps one after another with svgtune::

    %file figure.svg
    layers style=display:none
    layer label:re=^base$ style=display:inline
    %save base
    layer label:re=^nwb$ style=display:inline
    %save nwb
    ...

Links
  come from the slide itself (hyperlinks on shapes or on text: Insert > Link)
  and from the configuration file, which can link a whole shape, or words
  and phrases of its text, by the shape's name.
Build steps
  are assigned by regular expressions on shape names in the configuration
  file: the first match wins; shapes in a group fall back to the step of the
  group; everything else is "base".  Consecutive shapes of a step form one
  layer, so the z-order of the slide is kept (a step can therefore have
  several layers: select them with ``layer label:re=^<step>$``).
The .svgtune file
  shows base and then each step, in the order of ``walkthrough`` in the
  configuration, else of ``steps`` (whose order also decides which step a
  shape matches first), else as they appear in the slide.  It is
  regenerated on every run as long as its first line is the "Generated by"
  comment: remove that line to keep your edits.

The configuration (YAML; every key is optional, paths are relative to it)::

    source: figure.pptx          # the presentation (or give it on the command line)
    output: figure.svg           # default: <source>.svg (<source>-slide<N>.svg for slide N > 1)
    slide: 1                     # which slide, counting from 1 in presentation order
    title: "Figure 1"            # <title> of the SVG (default: name of the source)
    links:                       # shape name -> URL, or phrase -> URL
      EMBER: https://emberarchive.org
      HDF5 Zarr:                 # links on words or phrases of the shape's text
        HDF5: https://www.hdfgroup.org/solutions/hdf5/
        Zarr: https://zarr.dev
        _: https://...           # optional: the rest of the shape
    steps:                       # build steps: [name, regex on shape names]; first match wins
      - [nwb, '^NWB']
      - [bids, '^BIDS']
    walkthrough: [nwb, bids]     # order of the steps in the .svgtune (default: as in steps)
    split:                       # cut a straight line into segments of different steps
      Path B: {at: [449.3], steps: [bids-format, bids-dataset]}

Supported: shapes with preset geometry rect, roundRect, ellipse, and line;
straight and elbow (bentConnector2, bentConnector3) connectors, with arrow
heads at either end; groups (flattened, with their scaling and rotation);
pictures (embedded); rotation; solid fills and outlines with transparency and
dashes, in colors given directly or from the theme (``schemeClr`` with
lumMod, lumOff, shade, and tint, and the shape styles of PowerPoint's default
shapes); text with font, size, bold, italic, color, alignment (left, center,
right), anchoring (top, middle, bottom), line breaks, paragraph spacing, and
wrapping as PowerPoint does, using the metrics of the font (or of a
metric-compatible one, as found by fc-match).  Gradient fills are drawn in
their first color.  Everything else (other geometries, tables, charts,
placeholders without a position of their own, text styles inherited from the
layout and master, effects) is reported with a WARNING, and skipped or
approximated.

Requirements: Python 3 with lxml and Pillow (for text layout), fontconfig
(fc-match) with the fonts of the slide or metric-compatible ones (e.g.
Liberation Sans for Arial, Carlito for Calibri), and PyYAML for a
configuration file.

See https://github.com/yarikoptic/svgtune
